{"name":"Gold Rush","hex":"#c4a777","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold rush","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any period of feverish migration into an area in which gold has been discovered.","A feverish obsession with seeking profits, especially in new markets."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_rush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold rush","blocks":[{"text":"A gold rush or gold fever is a discovery of gold—sometimes accompanied by other precious metals and rare-earth minerals—that brings an onrush of miners seeking their fortune. Major gold rushes took place in the 19th century in Australia, Greece, Venezuela, New Zealand, Brazil, Chile, South Africa, the United States, and Canada while smaller gold rushes took place elsewhere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/California_Clipper_500.jpg/330px-California_Clipper_500.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_rush","wikidata":"Q273182"},{"title":"Gold Rush (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold Rush is an American reality television television series that airs on Discovery and its affiliates worldwide. The series follows the placer gold mining efforts of various family-run mining companies, initially in Alaska, but then mostly in the Klondike region of Dawson City, Yukon, Canada. Prior seasons also included mining efforts in Guyana, Oregon, and Colorado. As of 2025, the show has aired 16 seasons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/e/ec/Gold_Rush_Title.jpg/330px-Gold_Rush_Title.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_Rush_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q1535987"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Wooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg/500px-Wooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Wooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg/1280px-Wooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Wooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg/500px-Wooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A man leans over a wooden sluice. Rocks line the outside of the wood boards that create the sluice.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWooden_gold_sluice_in_California_between_1890_and_1915..jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/NMA.0039209_Emigration._Sve